We have 6,800+ transactional/application records plus customer and branch dimensions, rather than simply 2,500 rows.

## Dataset Structure

| Sheet | Rows | Purpose |
|---|---:|---|
| `Customers` | 850 | Customer demographics, income, credit score, segment |
| `Loan_Applications` | 3,200 | Loan applications, approvals, amounts, interest, DPD |
| `Transactions` | 3,600 | Banking transactions and customer activity |
| `Branches` | 18 | Branch/location information |
| `Loan_Analysis` | 3,200 | Joined analysis-ready view |
| `Data_Dictionary` | — | Table documentation |

## Deliberate messiness included
You'll find issues such as:
- Missing income values
- Missing credit scores
- Missing occupations
- Bengaluru / Bangalore inconsistency
- Male / male inconsistency
- Mixed loan-status capitalization
- Personal Loan vs Personal
- Interest rates stored partly as numbers and partly as "8.8%"
- Mixed date formats
- Amounts stored as text in some records
- Missing values
- Multiple customer records across transactions/loans
- Different application channels
- Different branch types
- Realistic loan defaults and overdue days

This gives me opportunities to demonstrate Excel/Power Query data cleaning + SQL cleaning + Power BI transformation to build End to End Data Analytics workflow.
